# Zenith Printer — deployment settings.
#
# Copy this to .env and it is picked up by `npm start` and `npm run dev`
# (Node reads it via --env-file-if-exists). In production you would more likely
# set these in the systemd unit instead; either works, and the unit wins because
# a real environment variable overrides the file.
#
# The test suite deliberately reads NONE of this. Server tests build the app
# with explicit dependencies — `buildApp({ db, sheets, ... })` — so that a test
# passes or fails for the same reason on every machine. A test that read this
# file would pass on yours and fail in CI, or worse, quietly reach the real
# Google.

# Where the service listens. 0.0.0.0 makes it reachable from the LAN.
ZENITH_HOST=0.0.0.0
ZENITH_PORT=3000

# SQLite database and uploaded images. Both default to ./data.
# ZENITH_DB=/var/lib/zenith/zenith.db
# ZENITH_UPLOADS=/var/lib/zenith/uploads

# error | warn | info | debug
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Where the built browser assets are served from. Only worth setting when they
# live somewhere other than the checkout — a container that copies the frontend
# to its own path, say.
# ZENITH_WEB_ROOT=/usr/share/zenith/web

# Run every motion except the printing itself: jobs queue, render and complete,
# and no paper moves. For a demo, or a check that must not consume stock.
#
# The test suite turns this on for itself and does not read this file. A suite
# that reached a printer which happened to be plugged in would produce labels
# nobody asked for, and that is discovered after the fact or not at all.
#
# ZENITH_DRY_RUN=1

# The language the CLI answers in — the web UI follows the browser instead, and
# ignores this. Set it once here rather than passing a flag on every command.
# ZENITH_LANG=zh-CN

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Sheets data sources (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Path to a Google service-account key file. Leave unset to turn the feature
# off entirely — the "link a Google Sheet" entry point then does not appear.
#
# This file reads EVERY spreadsheet that has been shared with its account, so
# lock it down:
#
#   sudo install -o zenith -g zenith -m 0400 key.json /etc/zenith/google.json
#
# Share each spreadsheet with the key's `client_email` (Viewer is enough — the
# service only ever asks for read access).
#
# The library that consumes it, `google-auth-library`, is plain JavaScript: no
# native modules, no build toolchain, nothing to install beyond `npm install`.
#
# ZENITH_GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS=/etc/zenith/google.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Asset ledger data sources (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the ledger lives and the key that reaches it. Both, or neither: with
# only one set the integration stays off and says so in the startup log, rather
# than offering an entry point that answers 401 on the first press — a button
# that cannot work is worse than no button, because pressing it is the only way
# to find out. It does not stop the service: printing labels does not depend on
# the ledger, and a half-configured integration should not take the printer
# down with it.
#
# Leave both unset to turn the feature off entirely. The "connect to the asset
# ledger" entry point then does not appear at all, the same way the Google one
# does not.
#
# These are read here and never stored. A copy pasted into a data source would
# drift from this file the first time the address moved or the key was rotated,
# and the only symptom would be refreshes mysteriously starting to fail. It is
# also why a service with no authentication of its own can hold your key
# safely: it never accepts one over the network and never echoes one back.
#
# Which column identifies a row is not configurable — it is `sys_id`, the
# ledger's own answer. Refreshes merge on it, so a selection made before a
# refresh still means the same devices afterwards.
#
# NEXUS_ASSETS_SERVICE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080
# NEXUS_ASSETS_SERVICE_API_KEY=nxk_xxxxxxxxxxxx.yyyyyyyy
